===== 7721 Page 63 mssEC 12
740 pm  Baltimore Jany 23d 1865 
630 pm for CA Dana Our Agt at Benwood telegraphs at
four twelve this PM that he has dispatches following troops
trains this day ---- number one at five ten am
with sixteen cars troops, number two at eight twenty am 
with twelve cars troops, number three at eight thirty am
with ten cars troops baggage etc ---- number four at eleven
<deletion>fifteen</deletion> <insertion>am</insertion> with fifteen cars troops etc number five at one 
fifteen pm with thirteen cars troops &c ---- number six at 
two fifteen pm with twelve cars troops etc which includes 
all troops arrived at Belleair up to that time ---- he
adds that further arrivals are expected at five this pm
---- twenty six hundred troops have been transferred and forwarded
---- The Agt at Wheeling telegraphs that the movement is
working finely and that Schofield and two hundred of his
party were despatched at eleven twenty am and that the
commissary arrangements are working satisfactorily J W. Garrett Prest


220 PM  Pittsburgh Jany 24th 1865
1130 AM Genl Halleck. Your telegraph to Genl 
Meagher has been shown to me
he left here yesterday. Orders
have been given as directed by
you. Troops have not arrived 
and am unable to say where
they are ---- O Cross Colonel
& Depy Quarter Master Genl P

===== 8538 Page 60 mssEC 13
1220 AM 24  Eastport Miss Jany 21st 1865 
Eastport twenty one Gen Halleck ----
your telegraph of the 17th just received ----
strength of twenty third corps about twenty
thousand ---- I do not know the number of
number of companies of Artillery to be supplied
with batteries sig ----
Geo H Thomas
Maj Gen


920 AM  Norfolk Jan 24 1865
Norfolk 24th for Comdr H A Wise Chf
Bu Ord Washn . 12 torpedoes of nine hundred pounds 
each with insulating wire are required by Comdr Parker
Comdg 5th Divn James river for immediate use 
signed D Lynch Comdr & Inspector of Ord


10.35 am  City Point Jany 24th 1865
930 am 24th GV Fox I would like to leave
Ft Monroe tomw eve or next day morning for Fort Fisher
. Can you not go ? I would also be
pleased to go on a naval vessel if it is
convenient to spare one Ocean transportation snow 
all employed carrying troops US Grant

(ahr) for Halleck
. I shall probably left here by 12 M tomorrow for
Fort Fisher . All troops of Schofields command 
may be forwarded directing to that point . Meighers
command will go in the same way in the absence of
further directions . If Schofield reaches Washn in time 
I would like to have him accompany me. US Grant 
Lt Gen

===== 8561 Page 83 mssEC 13
4 P. M.
Ft Monroe Feby 2nd 1865
4 P. M. for Gen Schofield Washn or Annapolis .
I arrived here today with despatches for you
from Gen Sherman will be in Annapolis tomorrow
morning (Friday) sig John F Anderson Maj & A. D. C.


710 pm 2d  Ft Monroe Feb 2 1865
Capt Blodgett a Q m Annapolis
---- Please inform Gen Schofield should he arrive in
annapolis tonight that I will be at
Annapolis to morrow Fryday morning with
important dispatches for him from Gen Sherman J.
F Anderson Major & A. D. C.


7 PM
8 pm  Annapolis Feb 2 1865
Gen D. H. Rucker
Telegram recd . The steamer Ariel can take
one thousand men & 60 officers The New York can
take 1500 . To put more on would crowd them
very much . Gen Meagher requests that steamers
which have reported be directed to take his command
G. S. Blodgett
Capt. A Q M

===== 7782 Page 124 mssEC 12
3.05 P. M 
City Point Va Feby 22nd 1865
22nd 130 PM Gen Sheridan ---- Paines Brigade of Cavalry
has arrived here from the Valley. Wickhams brigade arrived 
some time since. Two passenger trains and two 
freight trains are now run daily over the centr =
= al road there is also great activity on the 
Fredericksburg road many supplies have been collected in the
northern neck and many more are smuggled in
from Phila and other places and taken to Richmond
over this road. 70,000 pounds of bacon 
alone have gone to Richmond the last week over
that road. Cannot Augur send a force to 
break that trade up? It is reported that
all troops from the Valley except those brought here have
gone to Lynchburg ----
US Grant 


11 AM  Fort Monroe Feby 23d 1865
U S S. Rhode Island Hampton Roads 23d for
Secy Navy. The newberne has arrived from 
Cape fear river which place she left on the
twenty first inst ---- her Comdr reports that our
forces were within four miles of Wilmington ---- heavy
fighting was going on and our forces were still
advancing ---- Very Resp fully Stephen Trenchard Comdr


